insert into public.flashcards (id, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('fy-001','feynman',1,'1.2',$$Explain Zero Trust to a new hire in your own words. Why is "the network location" no longer enough?$$,$$Model answer: Old security trusted anything inside the office network. Zero Trust assumes attackers may already be inside, so EVERY request is authenticated, authorized, and encrypted based on identity, device health, and context - never on network location. Access is least-privilege and continuously re-verified; the control plane makes policy decisions and enforcement points apply them per session.$$),
('fy-002','feynman',1,'1.4',$$Explain how a digital signature works and what it proves, as if teaching a junior colleague.$$,$$Model answer: The sender hashes the message, then encrypts that hash with their PRIVATE key - that encrypted hash is the signature. Anyone can decrypt it with the sender's PUBLIC key and compare against their own hash of the message. Match = the message is unaltered (integrity) and only the private-key holder could have signed (authenticity + non-repudiation).$$),
('fy-003','feynman',1,'1.4',$$Explain why passwords are salted AND stretched (hashed slowly), not just hashed.$$,$$Model answer: Plain hashes can be attacked with precomputed rainbow tables, and identical passwords produce identical hashes. A unique random salt per password defeats precomputation and hides duplicates. Key stretching (bcrypt/PBKDF2/Argon2) makes each guess computationally expensive, so even leaked hashes resist brute force at scale.$$),
('fy-004','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain the difference between control CATEGORIES and control TYPES with an example that is both.$$,$$Model answer: Categories describe HOW a control is implemented (technical, managerial, operational, physical); types describe WHAT it does (preventive, deterrent, detective, corrective, compensating, directive). A door lock is a physical (category) preventive (type) control; CCTV is physical + detective/deterrent.$$),
('fy-005','feynman',1,'1.3',$$Explain to a developer why their "quick fix" must go through change management.$$,$$Model answer: Unreviewed changes cause outages and silent security regressions. Change management forces an impact review (dependencies, downtime, security), approval, a tested backout plan, a maintenance window, and documentation - so the system stays in a known state and problems are reversible.$$),
('fy-006','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain how you would tell apart a nation-state attack from organized crime, from the defender's chair.$$,$$Model answer: Motive shapes behavior. Organized crime monetizes fast: ransomware, data theft for sale, noisy smash-and-grab. Nation-states pursue espionage/disruption: patient, stealthy, custom tooling, long dwell time, targets chosen for strategic value not payout. Attribution is hard, but persistence, sophistication, and what they take are the tells.$$),
('fy-007','feynman',2,'2.2',$$Explain why phishing still works and the three layers that defend against it.$$,$$Model answer: Phishing exploits human trust and urgency, not software bugs, so patching cannot fix it. Defense layers: (1) technical - email filtering, SPF/DKIM/DMARC, link protection, MFA so stolen passwords are not enough; (2) human - awareness training and phishing simulations; (3) process - easy reporting and fast IR when someone clicks.$$),
('fy-008','feynman',2,'2.4',$$Explain the difference between a virus, a worm, and a trojan with everyday analogies.$$,$$Model answer: A virus attaches to a host file and spreads when humans share/run it - like a flu passed by contact. A worm self-propagates over the network with no human help - like an airborne disease. A trojan pretends to be legitimate software so YOU invite it in - like the wooden horse.$$),
('fy-009','feynman',2,'2.4',$$Explain SQL injection to someone who has never written code.$$,$$Model answer: Web forms pass what you type into database commands. If input is not separated from the command, an attacker types database syntax instead of data - turning "find user named X" into "find every user and their passwords." Fix: parameterized queries that treat input strictly as data, plus input validation.$$),
('fy-010','feynman',2,'2.5',$$Explain why segmentation is the mitigation of choice for systems that cannot be patched.$$,$$Model answer: If a flaw cannot be removed, remove the ATTACKER'S PATH to it. Putting legacy/OT/IoT systems in isolated segments with strict ACLs means exploitation requires first crossing a controlled boundary; monitoring that boundary catches attempts. It is a compensating control: risk reduced, not eliminated.$$),
('fy-011','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain the cloud shared responsibility model with a rented-apartment analogy.$$,$$Model answer: The landlord (provider) secures the building: walls, locks on the lobby, fire systems (hardware, hypervisor, facilities). You secure your unit: who has keys, what you leave visible (data, identities, configurations). In IaaS you furnish everything (OS up); in SaaS the landlord furnishes nearly all and you just manage who you let in.$$),
('fy-012','feynman',3,'3.2',$$Explain to management why an IDS alone will not stop an attack.$$,$$Model answer: An IDS watches a COPY of traffic and raises alerts - it is detective, adding no blocking. Stopping attacks needs inline controls (IPS, firewall) or a human/automation acting on the alert quickly. IDS still matters: visibility, forensics, and catching what inline tools miss, without risking blocking legitimate traffic.$$),
('fy-013','feynman',3,'3.3',$$Explain the three data states and why "in use" is hardest to protect.$$,$$Model answer: At rest (stored) - encrypt disks/databases. In transit (moving) - TLS/VPN. In use (being processed) - data must be decrypted in memory for the CPU to work on it, so protections shift to access control, memory isolation, and secure enclaves. Attackers target the state with the weakest protection.$$),
('fy-014','feynman',3,'3.4',$$Explain RTO and RPO using a restaurant that loses power.$$,$$Model answer: RTO: how long until you MUST be serving customers again - determines whether you buy a generator (minutes) or accept reopening tomorrow. RPO: how many orders you can afford to lose - determines whether you copy the order book every hour or every night. Tighter targets cost more; the BIA decides what each process justifies.$$),
('fy-015','feynman',3,'3.4',$$Explain why "we have backups" is not a recovery strategy.$$,$$Model answer: Backups fail silently: corrupted media, missed systems, encryption keys lost, ransomware reaching online copies, restores never rehearsed. A strategy adds the 3-2-1 rule, offline/immutable copies, documented restore procedures, defined RTO/RPO, and regular RESTORE TESTING - the only proof backups work.$$),
('fy-016','feynman',4,'4.6',$$Explain MFA and why "password + security question" does not count.$$,$$Model answer: MFA combines factors from DIFFERENT categories: something you know, have, and are. A password and a security question are both knowledge - one phished conversation captures both. Password + authenticator app = knowledge + possession: a phisher must also steal your phone, which does not scale.$$),
('fy-017','feynman',4,'4.6',$$Explain federation/SSO: what happens when you click "Sign in with your company account" on a third-party app?$$,$$Model answer: The app (service provider) never sees your password. It redirects you to your identity provider, which authenticates you (password + MFA) and returns a signed assertion (SAML/OIDC token) saying "this is Jamil, verified." The app trusts the signature. Central benefits: one strong login, instant company-wide revocation.$$),
('fy-018','feynman',4,'4.8',$$Walk through the incident response lifecycle for a ransomware hit, phase by phase.$$,$$Model answer: Preparation (plans, backups, contacts) was done before. Detection: EDR/users flag encryption. Analysis: scope - which systems, which variant, entry point. Containment: isolate infected hosts, disable spreading accounts. Eradication: remove malware, close the entry vulnerability, reset credentials. Recovery: restore from clean backups, monitor closely. Lessons learned: fix root causes, update playbooks.$$),
('fy-019','feynman',4,'4.3',$$Explain why a CVSS 9.8 on an isolated internal server can matter less than a 7.5 on an internet-facing one.$$,$$Model answer: CVSS scores technical severity, not YOUR exposure. Risk = severity x exploitability x exposure x asset value. The 7.5 on an internet-facing system is reachable by every attacker on Earth today; the 9.8 requires network access an attacker may never get. Prioritize by attack path and business impact, using CVSS as one input - plus known-exploited status.$$),
('fy-020','feynman',4,'4.4',$$Explain what a SIEM does using the security camera control-room analogy.$$,$$Model answer: Each system keeps its own diary (logs). The SIEM is the control room collecting every diary in one place, translating them to a common language (normalization), and watching for patterns across them (correlation) - one failed login is noise; 500 failures then a success at 3am across two systems is an alarm. It alerts, dashboards, and retains evidence.$$),
('fy-021','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain hardening: why does a fresh server install need work before going live?$$,$$Model answer: Defaults optimize for convenience: extra services running, ports open, default accounts, weak settings. Hardening minimizes attack surface: remove/disable what is unneeded, change defaults, patch, configure the host firewall, enable logging and endpoint protection, then capture it as a baseline and watch for drift.$$),
('fy-022','feynman',5,'5.2',$$Explain the four risk responses with one real example each.$$,$$Model answer: Avoid - discontinue the risky thing (shut down an unneeded legacy portal). Transfer - shift financial impact (cyber insurance, outsourcing with liability clauses). Mitigate - reduce likelihood/impact with controls (MFA, backups). Accept - documented decision to live with it (low-value system, patch scheduled next quarter with an exception).$$),
('fy-023','feynman',5,'5.1',$$Explain policy vs standard vs procedure using the "we must protect data" chain.$$,$$Model answer: Policy: "Sensitive data must be encrypted" (management intent - what/why). Standard: "Use AES-256; TLS 1.2+ for transit" (mandatory specifics - with what). Procedure: "To enable BitLocker: step 1..." (how, step by step). Guideline: "Prefer passphrases over complex short passwords" (recommended, optional).$$),
('fy-024','feynman',5,'5.3',$$Explain third-party risk with the "weakest link" argument: why does your vendor's security become your problem?$$,$$Model answer: Vendors hold your data or connect to your systems, so attackers breach THEM to reach YOU (supply chain attack). Their subcontractors inherit the risk transitively. Manage it: due diligence before signing, contract clauses (right-to-audit, SLAs, breach notification), independent attestations (SOC 2), and continuous monitoring - trust is verified, not assumed.$$),
('fy-025','feynman',5,'5.5',$$Explain to an executive why you want BOTH audits and penetration tests.$$,$$Model answer: An audit checks the paperwork and controls exist and operate (are we doing what we claim?). A pen test attacks the reality (does it actually stop someone?). Audits satisfy compliance and catch process gaps; pen tests expose exploitable technical paths audits never see. One proves conformance, the other proves resilience - together they cover both failure modes.$$);
